WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
Jesse Harris, Filmmaker: If you&nbsp;
probably picked any other career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.240
and you had spent a decade working&nbsp;
on it and were really good at it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.480
you'd probably be really&nbsp;
successful and have a good life.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:14.800
Film Courage: How much money has the first&nbsp;
feature that you made Living Life made in total?

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:22.160
Jesse: It probably hasn't made really barely any&nbsp;
money I mean it was they paid a small fee to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.960
to go kind of work on it when they first picked it&nbsp;
up and they paid to finish it and put it on film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.280
and release it and market it but I don't think&nbsp;
they ever even made their money back on it I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.520
it had a small theatrical release in&nbsp;
a couple cities it played you know on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.840
I think internationally in a few countries&nbsp;
in a few tv markets but the distributor was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.560
you know they were kind of this tiny little indie&nbsp;
distributor you know at the time back then there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.480
was all these little companies popping up and&nbsp;
I think they meant well but I don't think they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.880
really knew how to kind of get a film out there&nbsp;
and so I don't think it ended up really making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.280
very much money for them I definitely didn't&nbsp;
recoup the money that my parents had given me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:07.760
to to make the movie but again for me it was never&nbsp;
about that I mean it was they were never expecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.360
that in the first place I mean it wasn't like an&nbsp;
investment either they you know it was the money I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.280
would have used they would have given me if I had&nbsp;
you know gone to pay to a film school or something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:22.880
right and so yeah I don't think it made money but&nbsp;
it obviously made just so much for me in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.930
of it's what gave me the inspiration to start&nbsp;
this film festival for young filmmakers (NFFTY,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:30.560
National Film Festival for Talented Youth) which&nbsp;
then led to starting to do commercials which then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:38.160
led me back to making a film again and so it was&nbsp;
definitely a life-changing experience and yeah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.760
just an incredible thing that I was able&nbsp;
to pull off at such a young age do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.120
think a filmmaker should expect to make money or&nbsp;
recoup their costs for their first feature film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.160
it's hard to say you know I mean I think that&nbsp;
it all probably depends on the scale and size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:04.160
of the film I mean I think that ideally you&nbsp;
start something small and you build from there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.760
and obviously things are changing all the time you&nbsp;
know I think budgets are getting smaller because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.360
they're being forced to be because the people&nbsp;
are paying less for them your distributors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.920
but I think especially if you're going to make&nbsp;
a move for any sort of larger amount of money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.280
that isn't your own money you should try to&nbsp;
have the attention be to make money because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.720
you don't want to just be taking people's money&nbsp;
and with no intention to make it back obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:35.120
but I think the best thing would be to kind of&nbsp;
start something small that you know you could pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.880
off and make good but then would have the ability&nbsp;
to maybe make some money back you know I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.840
maybe you could find a contained film that would&nbsp;
only cost a $100,000 or $200,000 or something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.440
which is still a lot of money but it's probably&nbsp;
a more realistic amount of money that you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.920
make back versus a couple million dollars even&nbsp;
if you could get that money you know and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.240
but I think to make money personally as&nbsp;
a filmmaker that's a always a long shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.840
you know I think that's multiple films and&nbsp;
being very successful when you would ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.840
do that I mean I think right at this stage&nbsp;
for most filmmakers it's just you're just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.800
lucky to have been able to make the film you know&nbsp;
and if anything happens beyond that that's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.920
you know a wonderful dream and have it shown in&nbsp;
theaters as well I mean that's that's a rarity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:22.160
too yeah for sure I mean especially now more than&nbsp;
ever it's rare to get something in the theater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.480
and if you'd asked me several years ago I probably&nbsp;
would have been like that's the ultimate thing now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.480
it's hard to even say right because you probably&nbsp;
have a lot better reach if you were on something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.680
like Netflix you know it's not as theatrical&nbsp;
and there's a lot of issues probably with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.640
some of that distribution because&nbsp;
it's on there and it gets lost right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.960
it's not very curated but it's hard to say I think&nbsp;
there's gonna be a lot of change in the next few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.080
years in terms of how people see films and what is&nbsp;
the best way to get it out to people but of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:57.840
I like nothing more than being in a theater with&nbsp;
a bunch of people and watching it and kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.880
feeling with them so I would love to always be&nbsp;
able to have a film in a theater for sure so the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:08.960
filmmaker quandary if you want to make films you&nbsp;
may or may not make a living at it you may or may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.400
not have to take other jobs what are your thoughts&nbsp;
on that what's your advice to other filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.400
it's definitely the hardest thing about being a&nbsp;
filmmaker right I mean if you probably picked any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.440
other career and you had spent a decade working&nbsp;
on it and were really good at it you'd probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.840
be really successful and have a good life you know&nbsp;
whether you're as a doctor or business or whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.800
right whereas the filmmaker you could do that and&nbsp;
you could still be pretty poor after being pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.440
successful right or struggling to get by and so&nbsp;
I think it's probably one of the hardest careers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.440
you could ever pick because even when you get&nbsp;
successful it's still you got to keep going right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:51.040
so it's definitely not for people that want&nbsp;
the easiest life of course but I think if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.480
you are that kind of person and I'm one of&nbsp;
those people there's nothing else I would do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.280
I wouldn't know what I would do if I&nbsp;
couldn't make movies you know and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.600
one way or the other I know I'm going to figure&nbsp;
out how to do it and the hope is that you can kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.240
of keep building and get bigger I think things&nbsp;
like commercials and branded kind of stuff is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.280
a really good way for filmmakers to try to make&nbsp;
money and make a living doing filmmaking because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:17.360
there is actual is an actual business there where&nbsp;
you get paid you know to go and make something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:22.160
but you're still telling stories you're still&nbsp;
being a filmmaker so I think I mean that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:28.320
path I kind of took to be able to kind of support&nbsp;
myself while not having a day job or whatever so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:33.120
that's not easy to get into that either but I&nbsp;
think that's kind of a one way to go about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.400
but on the other hand you&nbsp;
know if you also want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:43.520
a writer or something like that maybe you should&nbsp;
take a normal job and then spend your free time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:50.000
you know or the time you can take off because of&nbsp;
that job to then just write or to focus on that so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:57.120
I don't know if there's one perfect way but it's&nbsp;
definitely not an easy career and I don't think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.560
you should go into it thinking you're going to&nbsp;
become rich and famous but because you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:05.360
do this because it's what you love it sounds like&nbsp;
you have to be prepared to be very resourceful and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:09.520
just not needing everything spelled out for you&nbsp;
sounds like you've pieced together a lot of things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:15.840
on your own yeah 100 I mean it's just all about&nbsp;
putting together that puzzle of like you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:20.880
what you can how you can pull something off and&nbsp;
then at the same time how you can kind of make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.240
a living and that still amounts for me you know&nbsp;
I mean I’ve just made a film but I still have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.800
do commercials too you know I'm trying to write&nbsp;
other stuff so it's I don't think it ever stops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:37.840
you know it's always just kind of this I don't&nbsp;
know what you would call it this dance I guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:43.200
of trying to figure that out but yeah I don't&nbsp;
know I couldn't do anything else I know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:48.800
did you ever have a day job for a short time never&nbsp;
like a traditional day job like the closest thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:52.400
was running this film festival which was a day&nbsp;
job i'd go in there every day and do it we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.480
an office for a while I mean like so that was&nbsp;
my day job but it was different because I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:59.920
created it it was something I was super excited&nbsp;
about it I don't think I could be the person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:04.560
who sits in the office and works for someone&nbsp;
else that's just not me especially this point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.320
I would figure out something else I would have&nbsp;
to do you know if that was what I had to go but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.760
again that just kind of fits with my personality&nbsp;
which is I don't like to like be forced to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:19.280
something I want to go and go and make something&nbsp;
so I think I would rather like I don't know become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:26.720
like a tour guide in some other country or like I&nbsp;
don't know do something totally random that wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:32.880
being kind of controlled and forced into a career&nbsp;
but yeah magician yeah maybe go back to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:40.720
what advice would you give to 17-18-19 year olds&nbsp;
on making their first feature film I think if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:47.280
were a really young kind of late teenage early&nbsp;
20’s trying to make a feature film I think my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.960
first advice would be is that actually the&nbsp;
best thing to do that's what I did and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:59.120
was the perfect thing I guess for me because&nbsp;
it led me to where I am now but I just wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:07.040
is it what's really going to help you the most&nbsp;
and so I think if you have an incredible feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:13.360
script that is super personal and is something&nbsp;
that could be made at a realistic budget and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:18.720
you feel really confident then go do that&nbsp;
for sure but maybe it would be better to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:25.520
make a short film or a proof of concept or or&nbsp;
kind of develop your skills a little bit more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:29.920
I think I had this idea when I was that age that&nbsp;
I just had to make a feature film because that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:33.920
what would legitimize me or allow me to kind&nbsp;
of go make the next thing and I think that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:39.440
partly just because of the era because short films&nbsp;
weren't really available because this was this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:43.120
before kind of online platforms to watch them&nbsp;
everything like that and so to be a filmmaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:48.640
was to make a feature film but I don't think&nbsp;
that's necessarily the case anymore I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:56.880
you can make a short film and it can create other&nbsp;
opportunities I mean even in the feature I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:01.840
made in order to allow me to make this I made a&nbsp;
short film as a proof of concept you know and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:06.640
I and that was essential I had to do that I&nbsp;
don't think I would have been able to do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:11.680
without that and so I think my advice would just&nbsp;
be to to really make sure if you're gonna put in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:17.280
all the energy is that is that the right thing the&nbsp;
other side of that is I know a lot of people just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:22.320
make short film after short film and never do a&nbsp;
feature or never take that next step so I also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.080
don't think you should just get stuck because it's&nbsp;
also a huge amount of work to do any kind of film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:30.960
whether it's a short film or a feature film it's&nbsp;
a huge amount of work so I think it's just about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:36.480
figuring out what is going to help you the most&nbsp;
excel and kind of make the next project from there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:40.880
and if that means making a feature then go do it&nbsp;
but maybe it means also pulling back a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:45.360
and taking time to write that really compelling&nbsp;
feature while you make a short or something else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:52.000
or a documentary I think that's if I was giving&nbsp;
this advice to myself maybe just to slow down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.800
and pull back even and I don't mean that and&nbsp;
lose that excitement and that energy but just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:00.640
be a little bit more strategic I&nbsp;
guess about what you're gonna go make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.440
I don't know that's not what I did so I&nbsp;
it's hard it's funny that I'm saying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:09.280
advice to people but I did the complete&nbsp;
opposite I just dove in and did it but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:15.920
I feel like that's what the more sane advice&nbsp;
would be to kind of take it a little slower well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:22.000
the environment has changed since 2003 when you I&nbsp;
mean your story is very unique I think it's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:27.760
interesting but I mean to have you know this thing&nbsp;
in the back of the variety or whatever publication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:31.840
that was to say hey we're looking for this the&nbsp;
whole thing is a very interesting journey but for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:37.520
today's temperament and costs and everything but&nbsp;
can people get stuck in this sort of short film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:42.320
just making one after another and never&nbsp;
progressing or is that not a bad thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:48.640
I mean I think people can definitely get stuck&nbsp;
making short film after short film I think if your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:54.240
intent is not to really become a career filmmaker&nbsp;
but just a lot of people make films short films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:59.040
and they have regular jobs they have other things&nbsp;
in their life they just love making films and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:01.920
there's nothing wrong with that like people should&nbsp;
keep doing that I mean everyone should make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:06.960
story if they're compelled to do it but I think&nbsp;
if you really want to be a director as your career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:13.600
making a short film after one after the other&nbsp;
forever is either a sign that if it's not getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:18.640
you somewhere else then maybe there's problems&nbsp;
with your your filmmaking or your your something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:26.240
there is not working or you need to reshift your&nbsp;
focus you know maybe it's finding a a story that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:30.640
even more personal or compelling or it's finding&nbsp;
something someone else wrote if you're not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:38.000
stronger writer or maybe it's making a documentary&nbsp;
I don't know or maybe it's taking that leap to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:42.960
then try to do a feature it's really hard because&nbsp;
it's like there's not one perfect path you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:50.160
and so it's hard to say but I definitely&nbsp;
think you you want to kind of have some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:56.400
sort of momentum going and if that's not happening&nbsp;
maybe you got to figure out a way to shift it up.

